dering whether to propose the du differential test file instead, but P8 says implementation comes before tests, so I'll stick with the implementation task. Now I'm drafting the final task definition with the proper structure, including the deliverable type, required reading references, and the du use case flow. pkg/format for human-readable output formatting and pkg/sys for cross-platform stat operations per the architecture guidelines. I'm also deciding to use filepath.WalkDir for efficient recursive traversal and handle exit codes appropriately based on whether errors occur during directory access. I'm catching myself inventing implementation details again—filepath.WalkDir is idiomatic Go, but it's still a specific choice I shouldn't mandate. Let me reframe R2 to just describe the behavior needed: recursive directory traversal that accumulates disk usage in the right units, delegating to pkg/sys for file metadata. And R3 shouldn't reference specific flags as a requirement group; those belong in the PRD, not as task requirements.\n\nThe constraint about 4 PRD sub-requirements is really about task scope—I shouldn't try to cover everything du does in one task. Since I only have one task allowed, I need to pick the core subset that makes sense, even if it means not implementing the full feature set.\n\nLooking at what's essential, I'd focus on basic flag parsing, recursive directory traversal with size accumulation, standard output formatting, and proper exit codes. The more advanced flags like --exclude and -P would come later. This core set should fit comfortably within the 150-250 line limit—roughly 30 lines for flag parsing, 40 for the walk logic, 30 for accumulation, 30 for output, and 20 for error handling.\n\nNow I'm checking the PRD reference details. The analysis shows only two orphaned PRDs, which means the du PRD is properly linked somewhere in the system. Since I can't pinpoint the exact file number, I'll reference the directory where it should live and no